# ---------------- Paper 3 ----------------
N["hist.interp.skills"] = dict(
    cover="What Paper 3 asks (FACTS.md): one unseen extract (at most 600 words, author not named), one 40-mark question 'What can you learn from this extract about the interpretation and approach of the historian who wrote it?'. Interpretation = the historian's main claims and conclusions; approach = what they bring (the questions they ask, their interests, ideology and beliefs). Why historians differ (the four syllabus reasons: fragmentary evidence; selection and interpretation; the passage of time and new evidence; the time and place they work in) and how approaches differ (questions, ideology, the link between approach and interpretation). Method: read the whole extract twice; find the main interpretation (usually two linked aspects, as the specimen mark scheme shows); test each paragraph against it; separate sub-messages; use knowledge to explain and test the interpretation (AO1 is half the marks); use labels only when the extract supports them; write about the whole extract. Levels: Table A (AO4) and Table B (AO1), each 20 (FACTS.md). Practice on a neutral example before the topics: e.g. two short paraphrased summaries of famous arguments about the causes of the French Revolution (Lefebvre vs Cobban), clearly labelled as summaries.",
    src="No extracts copied from past papers. Write short, clearly labelled paraphrased summaries of a named historian's argument (e.g. 'A summary, written for practice, of the argument of Alfred Cobban, The Social Interpretation of the French Revolution, 1964').",
    eg="Worked example: a full answer to 'What can you learn from this extract…' on a practice summary, with a Level 3 answer (sub-messages), a Level 5 answer (one aspect), a Level 6 answer (both aspects), and commentary. Exam practice: three short practice summaries with tasks (identify the main interpretation; identify sub-messages; explain the approach; which knowledge tests it).",
    hist="E.H. Carr, What Is History? (1961); Geoffrey Elton, The Practice of History (1967); Richard Evans, In Defence of History (1997): use for the nature of history, briefly.",
    pit="The command is not 'evaluate the interpretation' or 'compare with other historians': it is what you can learn about this historian's interpretation and approach. Never guess the author's name: the mark scheme does not reward it.",
    fig="Build the hist-sort widget here (see Widgets), with a demo set: statements to sort into 'main interpretation', 'sub-message', 'approach', 'not in the extract'. An SVG of the Paper 3 method (read, find, test, explain).")
