W = {
    "hist-timeline": "Generic interactive timeline (COURSE.md gives the attribute format). Attributes: data-title, data-events (events separated by ' ;; ', fields by ' | ': date (YYYY, YYYY-MM or YYYY-MM-DD) | short label | one- or two-sentence detail | optional lane), optional data-lanes (comma list). Shows events in date order on a horizontal (desktop) or vertical (phone) axis with year ticks; Previous/Next buttons and ←/→ keys step through events; the selected event's date, label and detail show in a readout (aria-live); lane filter buttons (.w-tabs, aria-pressed) when lanes are given; dates spaced proportionally but with a minimum gap so labels never overlap (or a list mode toggle). Bad or missing attributes: a friendly .w-err message. Probe with 3, 12 and 30 events and with long labels. Use it in hist.eu.france.2 (1789–92) and add a short demo of 1787–89 in the probe only (do not edit the gold chapter).",
    "hist-chart": "Generic chart of historical statistics. Attributes: data-title, data-unit (y-axis label), data-type ('line' or 'bar'), data-series ('Name: 1880=3.3, 1890=6.0, … ;; Second series: …', at most two series), data-source (shown under the chart; required). Canvas via W.canvas and W.axes; y-range from the data (start at 0 for bars); hovering or keyboard-stepping (buttons) a year shows the values in a readout; a 'Show table' toggle renders an accessible HTML table; describe() states the trend with first and last values. Probe with one series, two series with different magnitudes (note: one axis only, so pick comparable series), missing values and bad input. First use: Russian industrial output in hist.eu.russia.1 with a cited source.",
    "hist-sort": "Generic sorting exercise. Attributes: data-title, data-cats (comma list, 2–5 categories), data-items ('statement | correct category | short explanation ;; …'). Each statement shows with one button per category (aria-pressed); 'Check' marks each right or wrong with a word and symbol (not colour alone) and shows the explanation; 'Reset' and 'Shuffle'; a score readout. Keyboard only, no dragging. First use in hist.interp.skills (sorting main interpretation vs sub-message vs approach) and hist.interp.ww1.2 (schools of thought).",
    "hist-provenance": "Source-evaluation trainer. Attributes: data-question (the 'as evidence about…' issue), data-sources ('label | provenance line | short content ;; …'), data-answers ('label | stronger or weaker | reason ;; …'). For each source the reader chooses nature, origin and purpose tags from given options and judges 'stronger' or 'weaker' as evidence for the question, then compares with a model judgement and reason. Keyboard and screen-reader friendly. First use in hist.skills.sources with real labelled sources.",
    "hist-essayplan": "Essay-plan builder. Attributes: data-question, data-factors ('factor | side (support or challenge) | evidence hint ;; …'). The reader assigns each factor to support or challenge (buttons), ranks factors with up/down buttons, chooses a criterion and a judgement from options, then 'Build plan' prints an ordered paragraph outline (intro line, paragraphs, conclusion) as text they can copy. No dragging. First use in hist.skills.essays.",
    "hist-levels": "Mark-band explorer from FACTS.md's descriptors (text must match FACTS.md exactly in substance). Choose paper and part (P1 a, P1 b, P2 a, P2 b, P3 A, P3 B, P4 A, P4 B) with .w-tabs; tick the features an answer shows (checkboxes in .w-ctl rows); the widget shows the best-fit level and mark range with the descriptor, and what would move it up one level. Note on screen: 'Examiners use best fit; this is a guide.' First use in hist.exam.p1 and hist.exam.p2."}
